---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="Home">

<!-- ══ HERO ══ -->
<section class="home-hero">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="hero-eyebrow">Based on Mu. Varadarajan's Tamil Ilakkiya Varalaru</div>
    <h1 class="home-title">
      <span class="hero-title-ta">தமிழ் இலக்கிய வரலாறு</span>
      <span class="hero-title-en">A History of Tamil Literature</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="home-lead">
      Tamil literature does not begin with a single text or a single author. It begins with a claim — the legend of three academies, lasting thousands of years, their cities swallowed by the sea. What survives from the third academy is the largest body of ancient secular poetry in any Indian language, composed by hundreds of named poets across six centuries, in a language still spoken by eighty million people today.
    </p>
    <p class="home-lead">
      This is not a survey. It is a study — of the people who made the literature, the politics that shaped it, the beliefs it encodes, and its place in world literary history. No softening. No nationalism. No mythology passed off as history.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-cta-row">
      <a href="/history" class="btn btn--primary">Begin the History</a>
      <a href="/works" class="btn btn--outline">Browse the Works</a>
    </div>
    <hr class="rule-sm" style="margin-top: 3rem;" />
    <div class="stat-row">
      <div class="stat"><span class="stat-num">2,000+</span><span class="stat-label">Years of unbroken literature</span></div>
      <div class="stat-sep"></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="stat-num">473+</span><span class="stat-label">Named Sangam poets</span></div>
      <div class="stat-sep"></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="stat-num">30+</span><span class="stat-label">Named women poets, Sangam era alone</span></div>
      <div class="stat-sep"></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="stat-num">80M</span><span class="stat-label">Tamil speakers today</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══ OPENING VERSE ══ -->
<section class="home-verse-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="home-verse-inner prose-wide">
      <p class="home-verse-intro">The most quoted Tamil poem of the last two thousand years. One poet. One poem. Fourteen lines. Written in the first or second century CE — contemporary with Marcus Aurelius, before Beowulf existed, before any European vernacular literature had a name.</p>
      <div class="verse-block">
        <div class="verse-tamil">யாதும் ஊரே யாவரும் கேளிர்
தீதும் நன்றும் பிறர்தர வாரா
நோதலும் தணிதலும் அவற்றோரன்ன
சாதலும் புதுவது அன்றே.</div>
        <div class="verse-translation">"Every town is my own; every person is kin.<br/>
Good and evil come not from others — they are within.<br/>
Pain and its relief are like that too.<br/>
Death itself is nothing new."</div>
        <div class="verse-attribution">— Kaniyan Pungundranar, Purananuru 192 · <em>~1st–2nd century CE</em></div>
      </div>
      <p class="verse-note">This poem was written while Rome still stood. It predates the Quran by five centuries, the Canterbury Tales by twelve. It has been quoted by anti-colonial nationalists, by Tamil Eelam politicians, by Indian prime ministers, and by scholars of ancient cosmopolitanism. It belongs to all of them and to none of them. It belongs to the language.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══ SIX PERIODS ══ -->
<section class="home-periods-section">
  <div class="container">
    <hr class="rule" />
    <div class="section-label">The Literary Periods</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Two Thousand Years in Six Chapters</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Mu. Varadarajan's framework, followed throughout this site. Each period has its own politics, its own social conditions, its own literature — and its own silences.</p>

    <div class="periods-list">

      <a href="/history#three-sangams" class="period-row reveal">
        <div class="pr-date">Legendary</div>
        <div class="pr-body">
          <h3 class="pr-title">The Three Sangams <span class="pr-ta">முச்சங்கம்</span></h3>
          <p class="pr-desc">Three literary academies, each lasting thousands of years, each city swallowed by the sea. Tamil civilization's founding myth — and its founding political claim. The Pandya kings are written into the origin of Tamil literature itself.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pr-arrow">→</div>
      </a>

      <a href="/sangam" class="period-row reveal">
        <div class="pr-date">300 BCE – 300 CE</div>
        <div class="pr-body">
          <h3 class="pr-title">The Sangam Age <span class="pr-ta">சங்க காலம்</span></h3>
          <p class="pr-desc">The Eight Anthologies and Ten Idylls. 473 named poets. 30+ named women. Roman coins in Tamil soil. A civilization at the height of its trade wealth, composing secular, humanist poetry of extraordinary quality — while the Roman Empire was still intact.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pr-arrow">→</div>
      </a>

      <a href="/history#kalabhra" class="period-row reveal">
        <div class="pr-date">300 CE – 600 CE</div>
        <div class="pr-body">
          <h3 class="pr-title">The Kalabhra Gap <span class="pr-ta">கலப்பிரர் இடைவெளி</span></h3>
          <p class="pr-desc">The period Tamil tradition calls a dark age. It was not. The Kalabhras displaced the crowned kings and patronised Jainism and Buddhism. Under their rule: Silappadikaram, Manimekalai, and the Thirukkural. The "darkness" was Brahminic authority being displaced. That is a different thing.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pr-arrow">→</div>
      </a>

      <a href="/epics" class="period-row reveal">
        <div class="pr-date">200 CE – 900 CE</div>
        <div class="pr-body">
          <h3 class="pr-title">The Five Great Epics <span class="pr-ta">ஐம்பெரும் காப்பியங்கள்</span></h3>
          <p class="pr-desc">Silappadikaram, Manimekalai, Civaka Cintamani, Valayapathi, Kundalakesi. Jain, Buddhist, and emerging Shaiva. A woman who burns a city for justice. A Buddhist saint who feeds the hungry with a bowl that never empties. This is what the post-Sangam world produced.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pr-arrow">→</div>
      </a>

      <a href="/history#bhakti-age" class="period-row reveal">
        <div class="pr-date">600 CE – 900 CE</div>
        <div class="pr-body">
          <h3 class="pr-title">The Bhakti Age <span class="pr-ta">பக்தி காலம்</span></h3>
          <p class="pr-desc">63 Nayanmar saints. 12 Alvar saints. The most emotionally intense devotional poetry in any language. Also: the cultural reconquest of Tamil Nadu after the Kalabhra period, Jain temples destroyed, Shaiva legitimacy restored by royal command. Both things are true.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pr-arrow">→</div>
      </a>

      <a href="/history#medieval-age" class="period-row reveal">
        <div class="pr-date">900 CE – 1300 CE</div>
        <div class="pr-body">
          <h3 class="pr-title">The Medieval Period <span class="pr-ta">இடைக்கால இலக்கியம்</span></h3>
          <p class="pr-desc">The Chola empire at its height. Rajaraja I builds Thanjavur. Rajendra I's navy raids Sumatra. Kambar reimagines the Ramayana in 10,000 Tamil verses. Literature becomes imperial art — and retains its ambiguity despite the empire's needs.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pr-arrow">→</div>
      </a>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══ FEATURED WORKS ══ -->
<section class="home-works-section">
  <div class="container">
    <hr class="rule" />
    <div class="section-label">The Canon</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Start with These Works</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Each work page includes full structure, key poems from the TN school syllabus with line-by-line commentary, political context, and direct links to the full text on Project Madurai.</p>
    <div class="card-grid">

      <a href="/works/tolkappiyam" class="card reveal">
        <div class="card-eyebrow">~300 BCE · Grammar & Poetics</div>
        <h3 class="card-title">Tolkappiyam</h3>
        <p class="card-tamil">தொல்காப்பியம்</p>
        <p class="card-body">The oldest surviving Tamil text. A grammar that is also a complete theory of aesthetics. Establishes the tinai system — five landscapes, five emotional registers — that organises all Sangam poetry. Has no equivalent in any ancient literature.</p>
        <span class="card-link">Read the full study →</span>
      </a>

      <a href="/works/purananuru" class="card reveal">
        <div class="card-eyebrow">~1st–3rd century CE · Anthology</div>
        <h3 class="card-title">Purananuru</h3>
        <p class="card-tamil">புறநானூறு</p>
        <p class="card-body">400 poems. 150 named poets. The most politically important Tamil anthology — a record of a civilization at war with itself and at peace with the universe. Contains Purananuru 192: the most quoted Tamil poem of the last two thousand years.</p>
        <span class="card-link">Read the full study →</span>
      </a>

      <a href="/works/thirukkural" class="card reveal">
        <div class="card-eyebrow">~1st century CE · Couplets</div>
        <h3 class="card-title">Thirukkural</h3>
        <p class="card-tamil">திருக்குறள்</p>
        <p class="card-body">1,330 couplets on virtue, statecraft, and love. Translated into over 80 languages. Claimed by every religious community and every political tradition. Its silence on caste, its Jain-inflected ethics, and its unnamed God are all deliberate — and all contested.</p>
        <span class="card-link">Read the full study →</span>
      </a>

      <a href="/works/silappadikaram" class="card reveal">
        <div class="card-eyebrow">~2nd–5th century CE · Epic</div>
        <h3 class="card-title">Silappadikaram</h3>
        <p class="card-tamil">சிலப்பதிகாரம்</p>
        <p class="card-body">The Tale of the Anklet. Kannagi's husband is falsely executed by the Pandya king. She proves his innocence. She tears off her breast and throws it at Madurai. The city burns. This is a theory of state legitimacy — in 5,730 verses.</p>
        <span class="card-link">Read the full study →</span>
      </a>

    </div>
    <div style="margin-top: 2rem;">
      <a href="/works" class="btn btn--outline">All literary works →</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══ FEATURED POETS ══ -->
<section class="home-poets-section">
  <div class="container">
    <hr class="rule" />
    <div class="section-label">The Voices</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">The Poets</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Not just names and dates. Full studies of who these people were, what world they lived in, what they believed, and how their writing still functions as a political document today.</p>

    <div class="poets-feature-list">

      <a href="/poets/kapilar" class="poet-feature reveal">
        <div class="pf-left">
          <div class="pf-name">Kapilar</div>
          <div class="pf-tamil">கபிலர்</div>
          <div class="pf-era">Sangam Age · ~1st–2nd century CE</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-right">
          <p>The most prolific Sangam poet. A Brahmin who gave absolute loyalty to a minor chieftain, Pari, and composed ten elegies after the three crowned kings conspired to kill him. He then walked into fire. His grief is in the poems — not as performance but as testimony.</p>
        </div>
      </a>

      <a href="/poets/kaniyan-pungundranar" class="poet-feature reveal">
        <div class="pf-left">
          <div class="pf-name">Kaniyan Pungundranar</div>
          <div class="pf-tamil">கணியன் பூங்குன்றனார்</div>
          <div class="pf-era">Sangam Age · ~1st–2nd century CE</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-right">
          <p>One poem. Fourteen lines. "Every town is my own; every person is kin." That is all we know of him. It is enough. The poem has been quoted by Nehru, by Periyar, by Tamil Eelam activists, by scholars of Stoic philosophy. One poem, every generation.</p>
        </div>
      </a>

      <a href="/poets/thiruvalluvar" class="poet-feature reveal">
        <div class="pf-left">
          <div class="pf-name">Thiruvalluvar</div>
          <div class="pf-tamil">திருவள்ளுவர்</div>
          <div class="pf-era">Post-Sangam · ~1st century CE</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-right">
          <p>Author of the Thirukkural. We know his text completely. We know almost nothing about the man. Every religious community claims him. Every political tradition uses him. The text's careful silence on God's name, on caste, on sectarian theology is not ambiguity — it is strategy.</p>
        </div>
      </a>

      <a href="/poets/manikkavacakar" class="poet-feature reveal">
        <div class="pf-left">
          <div class="pf-name">Manikkavacakar</div>
          <div class="pf-tamil">மாணிக்கவாசகர்</div>
          <div class="pf-era">Bhakti Age · ~9th century CE</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-right">
          <p>A prime minister who gave the royal treasury to a Shaiva teacher instead of buying horses as the king commanded. The Thiruvasagam is the record of a man who gave up everything and does not know whether it was worth it. That uncertainty makes it the most honest devotional poetry in Tamil.</p>
        </div>
      </a>

    </div>
    <div style="margin-top: 2rem;">
      <a href="/poets" class="btn btn--outline">All poet profiles →</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══ THE ARGUMENT ══ -->
<section class="home-argument-section">
  <div class="container">
    <hr class="rule" />
    <div class="home-argument prose-wide">
      <div class="section-label">Why This Matters</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Tamil and the World</h2>
      <p>When Sangam poetry was being composed, Rome was active. When the Thirukkural was written, the Western Roman Empire had not yet fallen. When Silappadikaram was compiled, Western Rome had fallen and Latin was dying as a spoken language. When Manikkavacakar wrote the Thiruvasagam, Europe was producing almost no original vernacular literature. When Kambar composed his Ramayana in 10,000 verses, the Magna Carta had not yet been signed.</p>
      <p>Tamil is the only ancient classical language — older than literary Arabic, older than any European vernacular, far older than modern Persian or Turkish as literary languages — that is still the living mother tongue of its people. It was never revived, never reconstructed, never borrowed back from a dead archive. It was never interrupted. The grandmother of the woman who reads this sentence speaks the same language as Kapilar.</p>
      <p>That fact is not an argument for Tamil superiority. It is an argument for Tamil's inclusion in the conversation about world literary history — a conversation from which it has been almost entirely absent in Western scholarship, and only partially present in Indian scholarship. This site is one contribution to correcting that.</p>
      <div class="argument-links">
        <a href="/history" class="btn btn--primary">Read the full history</a>
        <a href="/works/tolkappiyam" class="btn btn--outline">Start with Tolkappiyam</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══ SOURCES ══ -->
<section class="home-sources-section">
  <div class="container">
    <hr class="rule" />
    <div class="section-label">Sources & Access</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Primary Sources, Free and Open</h2>
    <div class="sources-grid">
      <div class="source-card reveal">
        <h3>Mu. Varadarajan</h3>
        <p><em>Tamil Ilakkiya Varalaru</em> (Tamil Literary History) is the primary scholarly framework for this entire site — for periodization, critical judgment, and historical context. The most authoritative academic history of Tamil literature.</p>
      </div>
      <a href="https://www.projectmadurai.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="source-card source-card--link reveal">
        <h3>Project Madurai ↗</h3>
        <p>Free digital archive of classical Tamil texts in Unicode. Every work studied on this site is linked directly to its full text on Project Madurai. No paywalls, no registration.</p>
        <span class="source-link">projectmadurai.org</span>
      </a>
      <a href="https://www.tamilvu.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="source-card source-card--link reveal">
        <h3>Tamil Virtual University ↗</h3>
        <p>Academic course modules, structured lessons, and scholarly commentary for every major Tamil work. Used for curriculum organisation and academic cross-reference throughout this site.</p>
        <span class="source-link">tamilvu.org</span>
      </a>
      <div class="source-card reveal">
        <h3>TN School Curriculum</h3>
        <p>All poems quoted on this site are drawn from TN State Board Tamil textbooks (Std. 6–12) and standard UG/PG curriculum texts. Every poem is marked with its grade level for easy reference.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

</Layout>

<style>
/* ── HOME HERO ── */
.home-hero {
  padding: calc(62px + 6rem) 0 5rem;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  background: var(--bg);
}
.home-title { margin-bottom: 2rem; }
.home-lead {
  font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 1.15rem; color: var(--ink-mid);
  line-height: 1.9; max-width: 68ch; margin-bottom: 1.4em;
}
.hero-cta-row { display: flex; gap: 1rem; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: 2rem; }

/* ── OPENING VERSE ── */
.home-verse-section { padding: 6rem 0; background: var(--bg-alt); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
.home-verse-inner { margin: 0 auto; }
.home-verse-intro {
  font-family: var(--font-body); font-style: italic; font-size: 1.05rem;
  color: var(--ink-soft); line-height: 1.85; margin-bottom: 2rem; max-width: 65ch;
}
.verse-note {
  font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 1rem; color: var(--ink-soft);
  line-height: 1.85; margin-top: 1.75rem; max-width: 65ch;
}

/* ── PERIODS ── */
.home-periods-section { padding: 5rem 0; }
.periods-list { display: flex; flex-direction: column; border: 1px solid var(--rule); }
.period-row {
  display: grid; grid-template-columns: 160px 1fr 32px;
  gap: 2rem; padding: 1.75rem 2rem; text-decoration: none;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-light); background: var(--bg);
  transition: background 0.2s; align-items: start;
}
.period-row:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
.period-row:hover { background: var(--bg-alt); }
.pr-date {
  font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 0.8rem; letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  color: var(--crimson); padding-top: 0.3rem; line-height: 1.4;
}
.pr-title {
  font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.25rem; font-weight: 400;
  color: var(--ink); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
}
.pr-ta {
  font-family: var(--font-tamil); font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--tamil-red);
  font-weight: 400; margin-left: 0.5rem;
}
.pr-desc {
  font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 0.97rem; color: var(--ink-soft);
  line-height: 1.75; margin: 0;
}
.pr-arrow {
  font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--ink-faint);
  transition: color 0.2s, transform 0.2s; padding-top: 0.25rem;
}
.period-row:hover .pr-arrow { color: var(--crimson); transform: translateX(4px); }

/* ── WORKS ── */
.home-works-section { padding: 5rem 0; }

/* ── POETS FEATURE ── */
.home-poets-section { padding: 5rem 0; background: var(--bg-alt); }
.poets-feature-list { display: flex; flex-direction: column; border: 1px solid var(--rule); }
.poet-feature {
  display: grid; grid-template-columns: 240px 1fr;
  gap: 2.5rem; padding: 2rem; text-decoration: none;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-light); background: var(--bg-alt);
  transition: background 0.2s; align-items: start;
}
.poet-feature:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
.poet-feature:hover { background: var(--bg); }
.pf-name {
  font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 500;
  color: var(--ink); margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
}
.pf-tamil { font-family: var(--font-tamil); font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--tamil-red); margin-bottom: 0.3rem; }
.pf-era { font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--ink-faint); }
.pf-right p {
  font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 1rem; color: var(--ink-mid); line-height: 1.8; margin: 0;
}

/* ── ARGUMENT ── */
.home-argument-section { padding: 5rem 0; }
.home-argument { margin: 0 auto; }
.home-argument p {
  font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 1.08rem; color: var(--ink-mid);
  line-height: 1.9; margin-bottom: 1.4em; max-width: 68ch;
}
.argument-links { display: flex; gap: 1rem; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: 2rem; }

/* ── SOURCES ── */
.home-sources-section { padding: 5rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
.sources-grid {
  display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(260px, 1fr));
  gap: 1px; background: var(--rule); border: 1px solid var(--rule);
}
.source-card {
  background: var(--bg); padding: 2rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.5rem;
}
.source-card--link { text-decoration: none; transition: background 0.2s; }
.source-card--link:hover { background: var(--bg-alt); }
.source-card h3 { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 500; color: var(--ink); }
.source-card p { font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--ink-soft); line-height: 1.7; flex: 1; }
.source-link { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 0.8rem; letter-spacing: 0.06em; color: var(--crimson); margin-top: auto; }

@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .period-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr 24px; gap: 0.75rem; }
  .pr-date { display: none; }
  .poet-feature { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.75rem; }
}
</style>
